# Go toolchain

Use the commands below verbatim. Do not invent flags; if you are unsure, run
`go help <topic>` or `go doc <pkg>` and read the real output before acting.

## Everyday loop

```bash
go build ./...                 # compile everything
go vet ./...                   # canonical static analysis; treat findings as defects
go test ./...                  # run tests
go test -race ./...            # required for anything concurrent
gofmt -l .                     # any output means unformatted files
go mod tidy                    # sync go.mod/go.sum with the actual imports
```

## Modules and dependencies

```bash
go get example.com/mod@v1.2.3  # add or move a dependency to an exact version
go get -u ./...                # upgrade dependencies (review the diff, do not blind-merge)
go get -tool example.com/cmd/x # Go 1.24+: record a tool dependency in go.mod
go tool x                      # run a tool recorded by a tool directive
go list -m -u all              # available updates
go mod graph                   # module requirement graph
go mod why -m example.com/mod  # why is this module in the build
go mod verify                  # checksums of downloaded modules
```

`go.mod` `tool` directives (Go 1.24+) replace the old `tools.go` blank-import hack.
`go get tool` upgrades every tool; `go install tool` installs them all to `GOBIN`.

## Correctness and security

```bash
go vet ./...
go test -race ./...
go test -fuzz=FuzzName -fuzztime=30s ./pkg    # fuzz one target at a time
govulncheck ./...                              # go install golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@latest
go test -cover ./...
```

## Performance

```bash
go test -run=^$ -bench=. -benchmem ./...
go test -run=^$ -bench=BenchmarkX -cpuprofile=cpu.out -memprofile=mem.out ./pkg
go tool pprof -http=: cpu.out
go tool trace trace.out
go build -gcflags=-m=3 ./...    # escape analysis
```

Profile-guided optimization: put a representative production CPU profile at
`default.pgo` in the main package directory and `go build` picks it up
(`-pgo=auto` is the default since Go 1.21; `-pgo=off` disables).

## Toolchain version

- `go.mod`'s `go` line is the minimum language/toolchain version and it also selects
  GODEBUG compatibility defaults. Change it with `go get go@1.N.P`, not by hand.
- `GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` (the shipped default) lets the `go` command download and run a
  newer toolchain when `go.mod` or `go.work` asks for one. `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`
  pins to the installed toolchain.
- Debug toolchain selection with `GODEBUG=toolchaintrace=1 go build ./...`.

## Modernization

Go 1.26 rebuilt `go fix` on the `go vet` analysis framework as the home of Go's
*modernizers*: `go fix ./...` mechanically updates a codebase to current idioms and
APIs without changing behaviour. On older toolchains use
`go run golang.org/x/tools/gopls/internal/analysis/modernize/cmd/modernize@latest ./...`.
Always review the diff and run the tests afterwards.

## Runtime settings worth knowing

| Setting | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `GOGC` | Heap growth target; `GOGC=off` disables GC. Doubling it roughly halves GC CPU and doubles heap overhead. |
| `GOMEMLIMIT` | Soft total-memory limit (Go 1.19+). Leave 5–10% headroom. |
| `GOMAXPROCS` | Since Go 1.25 defaults to the cgroup CPU limit on Linux and updates dynamically. |
| `GODEBUG=gctrace=1` | One line per GC cycle. |
| `GODEBUG=inittrace=1` | Package init cost. |
| `GODEBUG=schedtrace=1000` | Scheduler state every second. |
| `GORACE` | Race detector options (`log_path`, `halt_on_error`, ...). |
